Z||000000||86SE||04-24-2000||10:09 PM||||I just bought a cheap pickup ($100).  Its a '74 Ford F-100 with a 390 V-8.  It uses oil, about a quart every tank, but I think I eliminated most oil consumption.<P>Anyway, after it has been running for at least 5 minutes and gets shut off, smoke comes out of the left (passenger) side exhaust manifold where it connects to the crossover pipe.  And an occational drip falls from the flange where it smokes.  Then after it sits for about a minute or two and I start the motor, it just billows smoke for about 30-60 seconds.<P>Could there be a dead cylinder that fills with gas and oil and pushes it all into the manifold and then it leaks out after I turn it off?<P>Thanks for any help!||||reg||5
Z||000001||Oreif||04-25-2000||01:30 AM||||What color is the smoke? From your discription it sounds like the head gasket is leaking antifreeze. When you shut off the vehicle the pressure in the cooling system actually increases since there is no flow. If smoke is white it's antifreeze if it's blueish it's oil.||||reg||1
Z||000002||DJRice||04-25-2000||12:46 PM||||I thought old ford pickups were <B><I>supposed</B></I> to do that....||||reg||1
Z||000003||86SE||04-25-2000||06:19 PM||||It is a white smoke and it smells terrible.  But a black liquid that looks like oil but does not smell like oil drips out where it leaks.<P>I just added a gallon of water to my radiator and already its about a quart low (10 miles later)........||||reg||1
